#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048191 :topic/_next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049332 :topic/math-academy-id 1036 :topic/title "The Chain Rule With Logarithmic Functions"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050271 :topic/math-academy-id 1007 :topic/title "The Chain Rule With Exponential Functions"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050789 :topic/math-academy-id 305 :topic/title "The Chain Rule With Trigonometric Functions"}] :topic/id #uuid "86f36ac3-ad23-5621-9648-009d53c6f968" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096742 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096541 :question/id #uuid "b00f1213-cdaf-512c-9842-92cbee1ec63a" :question/math-academy-id "e-6100" :question/problem "Differentiate $y = \\cos^4{2x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as a composite function\n\n$$\ny = u^4, \\qquad u = \\cos{v}, \\qquad v = 2x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u} = 4u^3,\\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm d v} = -\\sin{v},\\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm d x} = 2.\n$$\n\nApplying now the chain rule twice, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}v}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =4{u}^{3}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡v)\\cdot (2) \\\\  & =4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)^{3}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)\\cdot (2) \\\\  & =-8{cos}^{3}⁡2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Derivative Using Multiple Instances of the Chain Rule"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096844 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096403 :question/id #uuid "74ffb6be-e709-5635-b6e2-98911c40a19e" :question/math-academy-id "e-184" :question/problem "Given that $y =\\dfrac{e^{3x}}{2^{x}+1},$ find $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "We use the quotient rule with the numerator $u(x)=e^{3x}$ and the denominator $v(x)=2^x +1.$ \n\nDifferentiating $u(x)$ and $v(x),$ we have\n\n$$\nu'(x) = 3e^{3x}, \\qquad v'(x) = 2^x \\ln 2.\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to differentiate $u.$\n\nNow, applying the quotient rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\t&= \\dfrac{u'(x)\\cdot v(x) - u(x)\\cdot v'(x)}{[v(x)]^2} \\[5pt]\n\t\t\t        \t\t\t&=\\dfrac{3e^{3x} \\cdot  \\left( 2^{x}+1\\right) - e^{3x} \\cdot \\left(2^x \\cdot \\ln2\\right)}{\\left(2^{ x}+1 \\right)^2}\\[5pt]\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t&=\\dfrac{3e^{3x}2^{x} + 3e^{3x} - e^{3x}2^{x}\\ln 2}{\\left(2^{ x}+1 \\right)^2}\\[5pt]\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t&=\\dfrac{e^{3x}  \\left( 3(2^{x}) - 2^{x} \\ln 2 +3\\right)}{\\left(2^{x}+1 \\right)^2}\n\t\t}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Derivative Using the Chain and Quotient Rules"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096845 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096404 :question/id #uuid "a2bb6a33-8a57-5b6b-9d35-cf611fbd5c9b" :question/math-academy-id "e-185" :question/problem "If $f(x) = e^{-2x}\\tan({x^2})$, then what is $f'(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "This question requires the product rule. Let $u(x) = e^{-2x}$ and $v(x)=\\tan{(x^2)}.$ Then\n\n$$\nu'(x) = -2e^{-2x}, \\qquad v'(x) = 2x\\sec^2(x^2).\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to differentiate both $u$ and $v.$\n\nNow, applying the product rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =(-2{e}^{-2x})\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{2})+{e}^{-2x}\\cdot 2x{sec}^{2}⁡({x}^{2}) \\\\  & =2{e}^{-2x}(-\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{2})+x{sec}^{2}⁡({x}^{2})) \\\\  & =2{e}^{-2x}(x{sec}^{2}⁡({x}^{2})-\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{2})).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Derivative Using the Chain and Product Rules"}] :topic/math-academy-id 1115 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048202 :topic/math-academy-id 650 :topic/title "Differential Operators"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048385 :topic/math-academy-id 752 :topic/title "Using Differentiation to Calculate Critical Points"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048441 :topic/math-academy-id 291 :topic/title "Differentiating an Inverse Function at a Point"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049470 :topic/math-academy-id 57 :topic/title "Implicit Differentiation"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049658 :topic/math-academy-id 620 :topic/title "Rates of Change in Applied Contexts"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049713 :topic/math-academy-id 1362 :topic/title "Differentiating Hyperbolic Functions"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050531 :topic/math-academy-id 798 :topic/title "Differentiating Parametric Curves"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050901 :topic/math-academy-id 284 :topic/title "Calculating Velocity for Straight-Line Motion Using Differentiation"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051036 :topic/math-academy-id 463 :topic/title "L'Hopital's Rule"}] :topic/title "Selecting Procedures for Calculating Derivatives"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048777 :topic/_next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050321 :topic/math-academy-id 3769 :topic/title "The Sum Rule for Indefinite Integrals"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050985 :topic/math-academy-id 1686 :topic/title "Differentiating Reciprocal Trigonometric Functions"}] :topic/id #uuid "bdc990ad-e824-598d-8c5d-0b62dd6e1501" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064178 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064052 :question/id #uuid "a9671d61-6b85-5d84-b49c-a6443569f6c0" :question/math-academy-id "e-382" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int 3\\sin{x}\\,\\textrm d x.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\int \\sin x \\, \\textrm dx = -\\cos x + C.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 3sin⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =3\\int sin⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3(-cos⁡x)+C \\\\  & =-3cos⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Sine and Cosine"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064179 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064053 :question/id #uuid "4c3342aa-dd1c-566b-9229-164e594c554c" :question/math-academy-id "e-383" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int (-\\csc x\\cot x)\\,\\textrm d x.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\int \\csc x \\cot x \\, \\textrm dx = - \\csc x + C.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (-csc⁡xcot⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\int (csc⁡xcot⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-(-csc⁡x)+C \\\\  & =csc⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Secant-Tangent and Cosecant-Cotangent"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064180 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064054 :question/id #uuid "3d2bde25-f8dc-5e62-9999-6593424f0ae2" :question/math-academy-id "e-384" :question/problem "Calculate the integral $\\displaystyle{\\int} (2\\sec^2 x + \\sec x \\tan x )\\, \\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that \n\n$$\n\\int \\sec^2 x \\, \\textrm dx = \\tan x + C\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\int \\sec x \\tan x \\, \\textrm dx = \\sec x + C.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\int (2\\sec^2 x + \\sec x \\tan x )\\, \\textrm{d}x& = 2 \\int \\sec^2 x \\, \\textrm{d}x+ \\int \\sec x \\tan x \\, \\textrm{d}x \\\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t&=2\\tan x + \\sec x + C.\n\n}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Secant Squared"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064181 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064055 :question/id #uuid "c2c57145-3988-5ce8-b13f-114bba6964f7" :question/math-academy-id "e-385" :question/problem "Calculate the integral $\\displaystyle{\\int} \\csc{x}(\\csc{x}+3\\cot{x} )\\, \\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that \n\n$$\n\\int \\csc^2 x \\, \\textrm dx = -\\cot x + C\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\int \\csc x \\cot x \\, \\textrm dx = -\\csc x + C.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int csc⁡x(csc⁡x+3cot⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {csc}^{2}⁡x+3csc⁡xcot⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {csc}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x+3\\int csc⁡xcot⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-cot⁡x-3csc⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Cosecant Squared"}] :topic/math-academy-id 285 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048577 :topic/math-academy-id 2793 :topic/title "Necessary and Sufficient Conditions"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050571 :topic/math-academy-id 3575 :topic/title "Applying the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus to Exponential and Trigonometric Functions"}] :topic/title "Integrating Trigonometric Functions"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050420 :topic/_next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048465 :topic/math-academy-id 268 :topic/title "Finding Trigonometric Ratios of Special Angles Using the Unit Circle"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050644 :topic/math-academy-id 986 :topic/title "Calculating the Equation of a Tangent Line Using Differentiation"}] :topic/id #uuid "476d32c8-5b16-54ef-9688-daca6c7937ad" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059675 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059503 :question/id #uuid "31b9e2d8-1458-50b3-a920-703476825420" :question/math-academy-id "e-3631" :question/problem "Find the derivative of $y =  3\\cos x  + 4\\sin x + 2x^3.$" :question/worked-solution "We know that $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x} ( \\sin x ) = \\cos x,$ and that $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x} ( \\cos x ) = -\\sin x.$ Applying these formulas, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3cos⁡x+4sin⁡x+2{x}^{3}) \\\\  & =3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(cos⁡x)+4\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(sin⁡x)+2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3}) \\\\  & =-3sin⁡x+4cos⁡x+2(3){x}^{2} \\\\  & =-3sin⁡x+4cos⁡x+6{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Derivative of an Expression With Sine or Cosine Trigonometric Functions"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059676 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059504 :question/id #uuid "ae922c00-2ff5-5a46-805a-62955bc5a08a" :question/math-academy-id "e-3632" :question/problem "Find the slope of $y = 4\\tan x + 5x$ at $x = 0.$" :question/worked-solution "The slope at $x=0$ is given by the value of the derivative $y'$ at $x=0.$ \n\nFirst, we compute the derivative, using the fact that $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x} (\\tan x) = \\sec^2 x.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4tan⁡x+5x) \\\\  & =4{sec}^{2}⁡x+5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(0) & =4{sec}^{2}⁡0+5 \\\\  & =4\\cdot 1+5 \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope is $9.$"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Derivative of an Expression With the Tangent Trigonometric Function"}] :topic/math-academy-id 280 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048813 :topic/math-academy-id 1109 :topic/title "The Product Rule for Differentiation"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049070 :topic/math-academy-id 4386 :topic/title "Infinite Sets"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050902 :topic/math-academy-id 281 :topic/title "Second and Higher-Order Derivatives"}] :topic/title "Differentiating Trigonometric Functions"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050789 :topic/_next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050985 :topic/math-academy-id 1686 :topic/title "Differentiating Reciprocal Trigonometric Functions"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051084 :topic/math-academy-id 1108 :topic/title "The Chain Rule for Differentiation"}] :topic/id #uuid "671f0549-be03-552c-a435-a204b8c7e733" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064281 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064125 :question/id #uuid "2bb17470-6365-5e08-b649-f9f42146a4c6" :question/math-academy-id "e-732" :question/problem "Calculate $f'(x)$ if $f(x) = 2\\sin (3x - 1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f(x)$ as a composite function\n\n$$\nf = 2\\sin u, \\qquad u=3x-1.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d f}{\\textrm d u} = 2\\cos u, \\qquad\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = 3.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡u \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Trigonometric Function Using the Chain Rule"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064282 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064126 :question/id #uuid "7f56b401-c800-5708-8978-b84ce3f542f5" :question/math-academy-id "e-733" :question/problem "Calculate $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}$ if $y = 4\\csc (5x + 3).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny = 4 \\csc u, \\qquad u = 5x+3.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u} = -4\\csc u\\cot u, \\qquad\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = 5.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{csc}⁡u\\operatorname{cot}⁡u\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =-20\\operatorname{csc}⁡u\\operatorname{cot}⁡u \\\\  & =-20\\operatorname{csc}⁡(5x+3)\\operatorname{cot}⁡(5x+3).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Reciprocal Trigonometric Function Using the Chain Rule"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064283 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064127 :question/id #uuid "373a63fc-3dae-5880-8a49-63b5c64888f3" :question/math-academy-id "e-734" :question/problem "Given that $f(x) = \\sin^3{x}$, calculate $f'(x).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write\n\n$$\nf(x) = (\\sin{x})^3.\n$$\n\nNow, we see that $f(x)$ can be expressed as the composite function\n\n$$\nf = u^3, \\qquad u=\\sin x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d f}{\\textrm d u} = 3u^2, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = \\cos{x}.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =3{u}^{2}\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =3(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =3{sin}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Power of a Trigonometric Function Using the Chain Rule"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064284 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064129 :question/id #uuid "4ebd0289-b278-5352-b7bd-10ae218c43fb" :question/math-academy-id "e-735" :question/problem "Find the derivative of $y = \\tan{\\left(\\sin(x)\\right)}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny = \\tan u, \\qquad u = \\sin x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u} = \\sec^2 u,\\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm d x} = \\cos x.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={sec}^{2}⁡u\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & ={sec}^{2}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Composition of Trigonometric Functions Using the Chain Rule"}] :topic/math-academy-id 305 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048191 :topic/math-academy-id 1115 :topic/title "Selecting Procedures for Calculating Derivatives"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048705 :topic/math-academy-id 424 :topic/title "Integration by Parts in Cyclic Cases"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048776 :topic/math-academy-id 1785 :topic/title "Differentiating Inverse Functions"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049070 :topic/math-academy-id 4386 :topic/title "Infinite Sets"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049486 :topic/math-academy-id 1181 :topic/title "Verifying Solutions of Differential Equations"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050400 :topic/math-academy-id 1282 :topic/title "Calculating Derivatives From Data Using the Chain Rule"}] :topic/title "The Chain Rule With Trigonometric Functions"}]}
